WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.440
The U.S. government is really&nbsp;
worried about China’s ambitions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:05.520
when it comes to making microchips.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:12.480
But so far - China has NOT gotten its hands on a&nbsp;
very important machine sold by just ONE company.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.720
In a previous video, I talked about&nbsp;
how Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:22.080
Company makes over 90% of the smallest&nbsp;
and most sophisticated chips in the world.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.880
Their semiconductor chips power everything&nbsp;
from fridges to F-35 fighter jets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.840
In other words, they make civilization function.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:33.360
Well, people who watched the&nbsp;
video pointed out that TSMC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.440
cannot make their chips without one&nbsp;
piece of equipment. And it’s true.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:45.680
TSMC cannot make its chips without a $150 million&nbsp;
dollar machine from a Dutch company called ASML.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:52.880
ASML’s high-end equipment prints the tiny&nbsp;
features that form the basis of TSMC’s microchips.

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:57.200
Microchips are made by building&nbsp;
up billions of tiny transistors.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:03.680
An ASML machine patterns the finest lines on the&nbsp;
microchips using a method called lithography.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.400
The projection system shrinks and&nbsp;
focuses the pattern onto a silicon wafer.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.960
After the pattern is printed, it moves&nbsp;
the wafer slightly and makes another copy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.680
on the wafer. They are built up layer&nbsp;
by layer, like layering a cake except&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:23.280
MUCH more complicated because this involves&nbsp;
up to 100 layers with nanometer precision.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.760
The more transistors can be added to a&nbsp;
chip, the more powerful the chip becomes.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:34.240
ASML is the only company in the world that makes&nbsp;
the machine need to print advanced microchips.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.360
It manufactures the equipment at its&nbsp;
factories in Veldhoven, Netherlands.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.800
The company was founded in 1984 as a joint&nbsp;
venture between the electronics giant Philips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:48.400
and the Dutch chip-machine&nbsp;
manufacturer ASM International.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.600
ASML is now an independent company.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.880
It has a monopoly on EUV lithography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:00.960
“extreme ultraviolet light” - an extremely short&nbsp;
wavelength of light that can create even tinier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:06.800
circuitry than is possible with conventional&nbsp;
lithography which relies on Deep UV. (DUV)

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:10.800
EUV light doesn’t occur naturally&nbsp;
on earth – only in outer space.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.280
So ASML must generate it somehow.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:20.880
To generate the light, a CO2 laser fires two&nbsp;
laser pulses at a drop of tin, vaporizing it. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:28.320
tin vapor becomes plasma which emits EUV light.&nbsp;
This happens at a rate of 50,000 times a second!

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:34.000
The machines are assembled in clean rooms&nbsp;
containing zero dust. These rooms are 10,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:40.720
times cleaner than outside air. Even the smallest&nbsp;
speck of dust that ends up on a wafer can ruin it.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:46.560
The company’s decision to switch to EUV light&nbsp;
in the nineties gave it an astounding advantage.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:50.480
It cost billions of dollars to make&nbsp;
the radical switch in light sources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.960
but the effort has paid off big time.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.720
ASML is now valued at around $193 billion dollars.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:05.200
The EUV equipment is sold to the largest&nbsp;
chipmakers in the world: TSMC, Samsung, and Intel.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.280
Every one of its machines has over 100,000 parts.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:15.840
They’re shipped in 40 freight containers,&nbsp;
20 trucks, and three Boeing 747s.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:17.200
Like the rest of the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:22.800
China also relies on ASML machines for Chinese&nbsp;
companies that are producing semiconductor chips.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.440
But in 2019, the Trump administration&nbsp;
successfully lobbied the Dutch government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.886
to block shipments of ASML’s most sophisticated machines to China, 

00:03:31.886 --> 00:03:36.240
and the Biden administration has shown&nbsp;
no signs of reversing that policy.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:43.040
However, ASML is NOT restricted from&nbsp;
exporting slightly older technology to China.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:49.040
ASML’s older deep ultraviolet (DUV) systems&nbsp;
are a generation behind the EUV ones but still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:52.320
the most common way to make less advanced chips.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:56.640
In 2021, 16% of ASML’s sales went to China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:03.040
around $2.2 billion dollars’ worth. The&nbsp;
company expects similar figures for 2022.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:07.840
Washington has been trying to hamper&nbsp;
China’s chip-making progress in many ways.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:13.520
When President Biden signed the CHIPS Act&nbsp;
in August, it included a caveat that if TSMC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:18.560
or other chip companies want billions in U.S.&nbsp;
subsidies for building new plants in America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:22.400
they have to agree not to sell&nbsp;
their most advanced chips to China.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:26.800
Washington has also been pushing ASML even to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:30.960
shipping its less sophisticated&nbsp;
chip-making machines to China.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:34.880
This could deal a serious&nbsp;
blow to Chinese company SMIC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:38.880
which has reportedly been making&nbsp;
7-nanometer semiconductor chips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:44.800
that are only a generation behind the 5-nanometer&nbsp;
ones manufactured by TSMC and Samsung.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:52.000
If Washington gets its way, even ASML’s older&nbsp;
lithography equipment could be banned from China.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:58.000
China could start producing its own lithography&nbsp;
systems if it wants to become self-reliant.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.840
However, analysts believe it&nbsp;
would take China at least a decade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:05.040
to build its own equipment&nbsp;
that could rival ASML’s.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:11.520
ASML’s CEO Peter Wennink believes it&nbsp;
will be difficult but not impossible.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:16.160
He says: "I am not saying it cannot be done,&nbsp;
because I know the laws of physics are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:21.120
same in China as they are here. Never&nbsp;
say never. They will certainly try."

